# htxdev.db and data/events.json are COMMITTED, as of 2026-09-18. D18.
#
# Both were held out of git while the database contained Houston Linux User
# Group's personal calendar entries. That group is now verified, which required
# reading all 110 events on its feed, and both private entries are in
# data/rejects.yaml, so neither reaches the database at all. `make run` also
# warns loudly about any event carrying neither a venue nor a description,
# which is the shape those entries take.
#
# They have to be committed rather than merely may be: Phase 7 runs the sync on
# a GitHub Actions runner, which keeps no state between runs, so a sync can
# only build on the last one if the database is in the repo. htxdev.db is also
# the only record of when an event was first seen, which cannot be rebuilt
# because feeds only publish what is current.
#
# Two rules keep it cheap, both now in place as of Phase 7 (2026-09-18). The
# sync workflow never VACUUMs, because rewriting the whole file defeats git's
# delta compression, and it carries one repository-wide concurrency group,
# because two overlapping runs produce a binary merge conflict nobody can
# resolve. If you add a step to .github/workflows/sync.yml, keep both true.
#
# ONE THING THIS DOES NOT SETTLE. The repo is private today, so committing is
# safe. If it ever goes public, every historical commit goes with it, and the
# reject list is reactive: it catches a private entry after somebody notices,
# not before it is committed. Going public needs a look at the history, not
# just at the current file.
